<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maule River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maule is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> that flows from the Andes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> at Constitución. Tradition holds that it marked the southern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, where Inca expansion stopped in the face of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> resistance in the late 15th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maule-river.jpg" alt="Maule River" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anaximander · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rio_Maule_aerial2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Maule, called Río Maule and named from a Mapudungun word meaning rainy, is about 240 kilometres long, and its basin covers around 20,600 square kilometres, of which thirty per cent lies in the Andean range. It rises in Laguna del Maule in Talca Province at an altitude of 2,200 metres, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. It flows westward and is soon joined by the Melado River, and further on by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, Claro, Puelche and Cipreses rivers.</p><p>Before it reaches the central valley the river is dammed into the Colbún dam lake, which feeds the hydroelectric power station of the same name. Further on it is joined by another river named Claro and by the Loncomilla, which receives the Putagán, the Perquilauquén, the Purapel and the Cauquenes among its tributaries. In its lower reaches it receives one modest tributary, the Estero de los Puercos, and ends at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> north of the city of Constitución.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river is linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>'s pre-Hispanic times, its conquest, its colonial period, its wars of Independence and its agriculture, including wine and traditional crops. It marked the southern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. The Maule Region takes its name from the river, and many people notable in Chilean history were born there. The Valley of Maule is a viticultural region, a subregion of the Central Valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Maule has been called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>'s literary river par excellence, and &quot;the river of the fogs&quot; has given its name to the region. Many poets, writers, historians, journalists, composers and folk musicians were born or settled in the Maule Region, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> stories have the river as their setting or protagonist. The Chilean wine palm, Jubaea chilensis, was once present in the basin, but the growth of the human population has restricted this endangered tree to a smaller range that still includes some of the northern part of the Maule Region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maule River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Túpac Inca Yupanqui defeated the Kingdom of Chimor around 1470 and campaigned south through Collasuyu into present-day Chile and north-west Argentina, where the advance stopped near the <b>Maule River</b> in the face of Mapuche resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Troops of the Inca Empire reached the <b>Maule River</b> and fought the Mapuche; most scholars place the Inca border between Santiago and the Maule, so most Mapuche escaped Inca rule.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mapuche">Mapuche · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
